WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:35.900
"Every lie tell incurs a debt to the truth.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.540
Sooner or later that debt is paid."

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:50.500
The central theme of HBO's dramatic miniseries Chernobyl revolves around how lies can lead to disaster.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:57.200
How the way we craft stories
about what really happened can cause harm.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:05.140
This however presents an ironic challenge 
when creating a dramatization of real events.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:14.220
Dramatic narrative by its nature requires modification and simplification of what actually happened.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:24.160
So how do you tell a story like
this in a way that isn't hypocritical?

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:30.340
Step the series takes to avoid
hypocrisy is the creator's attention to visual detail.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.920
Small details about how things appeared 
may sometimes seem inconsequential.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:46.060
But the series has an outstanding dedication
to portraying these details accurately

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:47.460
and I think it matters.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:52.900
"Because of the nature of the working area you
will each have no more than 90 seconds.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:58.920
Some of it is in blocks weighing approximately 40 to 50 kilograms. They all must be thrown over the edge here.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:06.200
Watch your comrades moving fast from this opening, then turning to the left, and entering the workspace here.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.680
Take care not to stumble.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.260
Do you understand your mission as I have described it?

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.220
["Yes, Comrade General."]

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:16.880
These are the most important
90 seconds of your lives."

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.260
The documentary footage I'm showing
as a comparison here was often used

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:25.360
for reference in designing
costumes, buildings, sets, etc.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:31.480
Most of what is changed about the story is shifting things on the timeline or condensing information

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:37.380
or characters in a way that is necessary
to tell the story in only five episodes.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:43.600
"Above all, while on the roof, throw your debris over the rail. Do not look over the rail. Is that clear?

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.860
Very well. On my mark. Ready.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:49.140
Go."

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.220
Seeing the show and reality side-by-side

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:56.360
makes it obvious how understated
the show's approach to the story is.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:02.400
There would have been many opportunities to try to make events look and feel more dramatic

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.260
but it wasn't necessary and I think it pays off.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:15.400
The significance of getting visual details
like this rail is that each moment of the show

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.960
is true to the experience of those who were there.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:25.420
Even when those moments might be shifted
in time or context for the sake of the narrative.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.940
"One of the atomic reactors at the
Chernobyl Atomic Power plant in the city of..."

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.120
[Speaking in Russian]

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:38.240
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
"I was always aware that I was telling a story that meant

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.040
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
an enormous amount to the people that lived through it.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.920
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
There are people alive today,

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:45.400
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
thousands tens of thousands people alive today

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:49.120
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
who have lost people they love because of Chernobyl

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.420
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
whose lives have been shortened because of Chernobyl

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.020
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
there are people walking a lot of people walking around

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:54.940
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
without a thyroid because of Chernobyl.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:55.560
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.320
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
And it was important for me to tell that story accurately.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.760
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
Our production designer, Luke Hull,

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.360
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
worked very closely with our costume designer

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.500
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
Odile Dicks-Meraux.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.220
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
We just became obsessed with

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:14.140
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
showing things as they were."

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:23.240
Most docudramas leave it to the viewer to
figure out what events and characters were changed.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.867
HBO's Chernobyl interesting link gives
you the tools to learn these things.

00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:33.340
The companion podcast covers much
of what was modified and why.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:40.940
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
"Of those, many wonderful reasons to do this

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:43.700
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
the one that was most important to me from the jump

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:47.200
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
was a chance to set the record straight about

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.240
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
what we do that is very accurate to history

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.040
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
what we do that is a little bit sideways to it

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.120
[Creator of HBO's Chernobyl]
and what we do to compress or change."

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.880
It's an interesting acknowledgement by the creators of the show of the way the meta discussion

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:10.100
and experience of watching a show about
true events is important to the story.

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:18.340
And being transparent and talking about these things and the process of adapting reality to narrative

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:25.440
is commendable. It sets an interesting 
new standard for films and TV of this genre.

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:35.340
"The real danger is that of we hear enough lies,
then we no longer recognizes the truth at all."

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:38.780
"What can we do then?"

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:44.160
"What else is left but to abandon even the hope
of truth and content ourselves instead

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:45.960
with stories?"

